  "notes": "\"He writes chords, unlike the snap producers\" is the sentence this entry is built on. So the harmony is stated 4-bar progressions — i–VI–III–VII and I–V–vi–IV, with major keys carrying real weight in `keys` — and `maxChords` is 4 against a lane whose parent tops out at a two-chord vamp. The melody is the pad-and-arpeggio writing the research names: 4-bar phrases, a bell lead, and a countermelody that leans on `arp` and `sustain_pad` rather than on an echo of the hook. The one drum parameter that is genuinely his is a mix decision — \"drum parts are mixed back relative to synths\" — which is authored as `velocityTiers` two bands below the parent's and a `percs.gainOffsetDb` well down, so the kit sits under the keyboards instead of over them. The clap is allowed on 2&4 as well as 3 via a moderate `offbeat8thShare` and a positive `layerClapOffsetMs` stack, matching \"clap on 3 or 2&4, handclap stacks\". The 808 keeps `role: bassline` because the research says the sub follows the chord roots — `followRootsProb` stays high and `slideProb` low. ⚠ `medium`: credits and era are sourced and an interview exists, but the research says in as many words that the harmonic detail is inference, and it marks both tempo bands LOW-CONF. The 118–128 straight pop-crossover lane is NOT modelled — this entry commits to the rap band (76–92 felt, stored as its 152–184 straight equivalent). The perc lanes and the ghost placements are INFERRED. ⚠ `fills.snareRollLadder` is off — both fill periods sit at 4 bars, the big fill wins, and the inherited climbing ladder reads no seed, so it would write one identical fill on all 200. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
